@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "The pattern";
}

<article class="guide">
    <p class="kicker">Guide</p>
    <h1>Long job → background worker → email with a session link</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        This project is a port of the original incomplete MVC demo into something you can copy into another ASP.NET Core app.
        The live site next to it is a different stack; this one is the .NET guide.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
        A browser POST that runs for minutes will be killed by Kestrel, IIS, a proxy, or the user closing the tab.
        The work has to outlive the HTTP request.
    </p>

    <h2>The flow</h2>
    <ol class="steps-guide">
        <li><strong>Start.</strong> <code>JobController.Start</code> writes a row, captures the public base URL, enqueues the job id, and redirects to Status. The request ends in milliseconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch.</strong> Status polls <code>GET /Job/Progress/{id}</code>. The worker is already running in <code>QueuedHostedService</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Detach.</strong> If it’s taking too long, POST Detach sets <code>IsDetached</code>. The page becomes Night shift. The worker does not stop.</li>
        <li><strong>Notify.</strong> On completion the worker re-reads <code>IsDetached</code>. If true, <code>IEmailSender</code> files a message whose body contains <code>/Job/Session/{id}</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Reopen.</strong> Inbox → Open session. The id is a capability URL; no login.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why the original demo failed</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><code>Start.cshtml</code> markup was truncated (<code>action="/Job/StartJoblass="btn…</code>) so the form never posted.</li>
        <li>Detached / Completed views had broken anchors (<code>/Home/IndexReturn Home</code>).</li>
        <li><code>JobController</code> injected <code>BackgroundWorker</code>, a hosted service. Hosted services are not meant to be resolved in controllers — DI blows up or you couple HTTP to the singleton worker.</li>
        <li>Email was <code>Console.WriteLine</code> to a fake <code>user@yourcompany.com</code>.</li>
        <li>The worker held a tracked entity across <code>Task.Delay</code> and skipped progress when detached, so a Detach POST could be overwritten and the session went dark.</li>
        <li>No layout, no polling, SQL Server LocalDB required.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The .NET pieces</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>IBackgroundTaskQueue</code></td><td>In-process <code>Channel<Guid></code>. Swap for Hangfire / Service Bus later.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>QueuedHostedService</code></td><td><code>BackgroundService</code> that dequeues, creates a scope, runs the processor.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>IJobProcessor</code></td><td>The actual work. Uses <code>ExecuteUpdate</code> so Detach cannot be clobbered.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>IEmailSender</code></td><td>Seam. Demo implementation writes Inbox rows; replace with SMTP/SendGrid.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Job.Session</code></td><td>Capability URL in the email. Bookmarkable results.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Do not</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Call <code>Task.Run</code> from the controller and return. App pool recycle loses the work; no DI scope; no shutdown drain.</li>
        <li>Inject <code>BackgroundService</code> into a controller.</li>
        <li>Keep a tracked EF entity across <code>await Task.Delay</code> if another request mutates the same row.</li>
        <li>Build the session link inside the worker with <code>IHttpContextAccessor</code> — there is no HTTP context there. Capture the base URL at enqueue time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Production upgrades</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Queue:</strong> Hangfire, Quartz, Azure Service Bus, or a Postgres outbox. Keep “controller enqueues an id.”</li>
        <li><strong>Mail:</strong> new <code>IEmailSender</code> using SendGrid, Graph, or SmtpClient. Leave Inbox as an audit log if you want.</li>
        <li><strong>Live UI:</strong> replace polling with SignalR; the worker publishes step events.</li>
        <li><strong>Store:</strong> swap SQLite for SQL Server / PostgreSQL in <code>Program.cs</code> only.</li>
        <li><strong>Auth:</strong> capability URLs are enough for this demo. For private data, gate Session with the owning user and still put a tokenized link in the email.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="actions">
        <a class="btn primary" asp-action="Index">Start a job</a>
        <a class="btn" asp-controller="Inbox" asp-action="Index">Open Inbox</a>
    </p>
</article>
